<description>
Extend each left-edge beam marker rightward, letting obstacle pixels create side wakes and split branches.

Input structure
- The grid has a dominant background, one seed color whose pixels all lie in the leftmost column, and one obstacle color.
- Seed pixels are horizontal emitters; all other non-background pixels are fixed one-pixel obstacles.
- Obstacles may appear on a beam row or immediately diagonally in front of another obstacle.

Rule
- From every seed, paint background cells to the right with the seed color.
- When a beam hits an obstacle, keep the obstacle unchanged and paint the available three-cell side wakes above and below it.
- A side wake is blocked if its left entry cell is an unactivated obstacle; obstacles reached inside wakes paint only their open side wakes and the cell immediately to their right.
- If a hit obstacle has obstacles at both forward diagonals, the original beam stops and full beams continue on those two adjacent rows without painting back through the parent obstacle.

Key insight
- The output is a directed ray simulation: obstacle pixels are fixed blockers that locally thicken the beam, and paired forward-diagonal blockers are the only places where the beam splits into new horizontal paths.
</description>
<annotation>
{
  "type": "move_along_path",
  "src": "left-edge seed pixels s in the unique seed color",
  "path": "horizontal rays to the right; a hit obstacle paints open side wakes, and if obstacles occupy both forward diagonals the ray stops and branches rightward on the two adjacent rows",
  "trajectory": "paint reached background pixels with s.color",
  "transform": "keep all original seed and obstacle pixels unchanged; side-wake entry is blocked by an unactivated obstacle and split branches cannot paint back through their parent obstacle",
  "multi_step": "simulate rays left-to-right from each seed; obstacles reached inside wakes activate locally but do not become full beams unless created by a two-forward-diagonal split"
}
</annotation>
<generation_spec>
```yaml
spec:
  grid: 6-26 rows x 6-26 cols, always square [observed]
  colors:
    bg: 0 (black)
    seed: 1 (blue), always color 1
    obstacle: 5 or 8, single non-bg/non-seed color
  seeds:
    count: 1-3 pixels, all in column 0
    row_spacing: >=4 between consecutive seeds [observed]
    each seed row has >=1 obstacle to the right of col 0
  obstacles:
    count: 2-80 single pixels, density 5-16% of grid area
    4-isolated: no two obstacles share a cardinal edge (one exception in test)
    diagonal adjacency allowed (chebyshev distance 1 is common)
    may appear at col 0 but never on a seed row at col 0
    spread across grid; larger grids use more rows
  rule: |
    From each seed, a beam paints rightward on the seed row.
    When beam hits an obstacle: paint 3-cell wake (cols c-1,c,c+1) on rows r-1 and r+1,
    unless wake entry cell (r+/-1, c-1) is an unactivated obstacle.
    For non-split hits, also paint cell (r, c+1).
    Obstacles found inside wakes activate recursively with local wakes.
    If obstacle has obstacles at BOTH forward diagonals (r-1,c+1) and (r+1,c+1):
    beam stops, splits into two new full beams on rows r-1 and r+1.
  branch_points: 0-1 per puzzle [observed]; formed by 3-obstacle triangle pattern

generation_strategy:
  approach: direct construction (forward simulation is deterministic)
  steps:
    1. Pick N = randint(6,26); grid is NxN, fill with bg=0.
    2. Choose obstacle_color from {5, 8}; seed_color = 1.
    3. Pick 1-3 seed rows with spacing >=4; place seed pixels at col 0.
    4. Scatter obstacle pixels with density 5-16%, ensuring each is 4-isolated
       (no cardinal neighbor is another obstacle or seed). Ensure each seed row
       has >=1 obstacle at col >0. Do not place obstacle on (seed_row, 0).
    5. Optionally create 0-1 branch triangles by placing obstacle at (r,c) on a
       seed row with obstacles at both (r-1,c+1) and (r+1,c+1), all 4-isolated.
    6. Run solver to produce the output; verify output differs from input
       (beams actually paint cells).
